soon after the shooting at Paul's  nightclub in 2016 a lot of gay and  bisexual men realized something they  left him frustrated they were not  allowed to donate blood our coverage of  the deadly shooting in Orlando and the  outpouring of support from people  wanting to help the victims Southwest  Florida man overcome with emotion in the  wake of Orlando's deadly rampage he's  now trying to help the surviving victims  who are fighting for their lives there's  one problem he says he's been denied  from donating blood  more than once because he's gay based on  the current FDA restriction men who have  had sex with other men in the past 12  months are deferred from donating blood  but when was this put in place and why  initially this restriction was  introduced in 1983 and until recently it  was a lifetime ban for men who slept  with other men at this time the US was  in the midst of the AIDS crisis and  there was still a lot of ignorance about  HIV and it's transmission which shows  that the lifestyle of some male  homosexuals has triggered an epidemic of  a rare form of cancer researchers are  now studying blood and other samples  from the victims trying to learn what is  causing the disease so far they have had  no luck one of the things they knew was  that this was a blood-borne virus and  the virus was affecting mostly the gay  community at this time with their  limited research and knowledge it made  sense to have a temporary ban on blood  donations from gay men but what does  this ban mean now 35 years later the FDA  updated its ban in 2015 changing it to a  deferral for men who have had sex with  another man in the past 12 months so  essentially if you're gay and you want  to donate blood you just can't have sex  for a full year so like a lot of us  can't donate but what are the actual  risks of having sexually active gay men  donating blood  well HIV is no longer a death sentence  and heterosexual people are also at risk  of contracting the virus men who have  had sex with other men remain at the  highest risk of infection in 2016 40,000  people were diagnosed with HIV in the  u.s. of those gay and bisexual men  accounted for 67% but science has come a  long way since the ban was introduced  donated blood in the u.s. is not only  tested to see which patient can the  blood go to but also to look for  possible diseases or infections that  might be present in the blood so in  theory most donations from people who  are HIV positive will show up in the  results and therefore  we'll make it to a blood bank but the  concern lies in what they call a window  period the window period is a time  between when the virus is contracted and  when it's a tech table the risk here is  that someone could contract the virus  and donate blood before it shows in the  test the reality is that HIV screening  tools have a significantly improved  since 1983 now we have a shorter window  period and more sensitive tests after  the window period has passed using the  testing methods of 1987 tests after an  eight week window show a chance of  infection in one out of every 150 3123  units of blood with today's methods a  risk of infection is one in eight to  twelve million units of blood so what  does this matter well as it turns out we  need blood in 2018 the Red Cross sounded  the emergency alarm for blood shortage  and these shortages are coming  throughout the country this constant  blood shortages are cured partially  because only 38 percent of the u.s.  population is eligible to donate blood  and only about 10 percent donate blood  yearly according to the Red Cross a  person in the US as in need of blood  every two seconds and one pint of blood  could save up to three lives lives like  Nathan's in Arizona Nathan was born on  August 24 2014 a healthy baby boy  three and a half months Nathan started  to look as if he had jaundice his belly  was really extended and I started to  question his appearance and how he was  behaving I took him into his primary  care physician they drew some blood and  I received a phone call around 11  o'clock that night telling me that my  son's hemoglobin was a four and his  spleen was in his pelvic cavity and I  needed to take him to Phoenix Children's  Hospital right away and we spent the  next nine days in Phoenix Children's  Hospital within that nine days Nathan  received three blood transfusions back  to back Nathan was having weekly blood  transfusions and they slowly spread out  to about two weeks and then eventually  three weeks now Nathan just received his  61st blood transfusion and he can go  anywhere between three to four weeks  before having to receive another blood  transfusion there was a time that we  were asked to come back the next day  because they didn't have his type of  blood in the blood bank in worst case  situations like natural disasters things  like that like that is a really scary  feeling because without blood  transfusions my son will pass away and  at that time it doesn't matter who is  donating the blood it's the fact that  we're getting blood for my little boy  and people just like him  I sign a document stating that I  understand that he can contract HIV but  the benefit of my son receiving blood  completely outweighs that risk could  because without a blood transfusion  there is no other way for him to be  alive if he will die so yeah I'm gonna  take that risk  I'll take blood from anybody who's  willing to get it and that means more  than anything in the world to me the  life of my son truly is in other  people's hands and that is the worst  feeling in the world to feel as a parent  now while the u.s. recently lifted the  lifetime ban there are other countries  that have screening processes that might  allow us to donate more blood in Spain  and Italy for instance each donors  potential risk is approached  individually to determine high-risk  blood donors for example if a gay man is  in a long-term monogamous relationship  he could be considere